<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=salt">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Farming and food</p><h1 class="enc-title">Salt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 6050 BCE</span> · Also known as Sodium chloride, Table salt</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Salt is a mineral composed mainly of sodium chloride, used as a seasoning and as a preservative for meat and fish. Some of the earliest evidence of salt processing comes from Poiana Slatinei in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, where people of the Precucuteni culture boiled spring water to extract salt by briquetage as far back as 6050 BCE. A salt works at Xiechi Lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanxi">Shanxi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, dates to at least 6000 BCE. Salt became one of the main trading commodities of antiquity, carried by boat, along salt roads and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camel">camel</a> caravan across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>. Most of the 300 million tonnes produced each year goes to the chemical industry.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/salt.jpg" alt="Salt" width="330" height="250" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James St. John · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rock_salt_(halitite)_(Billianwala_Salt_Member,_Salt_Range_Formation,_Ediacaran_to_Lower_Cambrian;_Khewra_Salt_Mine,_Salt_Range,_Pakistan)_14.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 6050 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Poiana Slatinei, Romania</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Also arose independently</span>Xiechi Lake, Shanxi, China (at least 6000 BCE)</p><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Solnitsata in Bulgaria is also said to have supplied salt from 5400 BCE; that is later than the Romanian evidence.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=salt">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
